- {"summary": "The paper proposes a framework for one-shot style personalization in RL, using Granular Ball methods to learn interpretable style representations. It enables adaptation to human preferences from a single demonstration and demonstrates strong performance in simulated environments.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper presents a novel framework for one-shot style alignment in RL, enabling agents to adapt to human preferences from a single demonstration. It contributes a robust and interpretable style representation using Granular Ball methods, with strong empirical results.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper introduces a novel framework for one-shot style personalization in reinforcement learning, addressing an important gap in aligning RL agents with human preferences (Section 1).", "The proposed method demonstrates strong empirical performance across diverse environments, including both single-agent and multi-agent tasks (Section 4).", "The use of Granular Ball methods for structured representation learning provides an interpretable and robust approach to style modeling (Section 3.1).", "The framework enables smooth interpolation between styles and generalization to unseen style combinations, offering practical flexibility (Section 4.4)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper lacks a thorough theoretical analysis of the proposed method's convergence properties or guarantees (Section 3).", "The evaluation is limited to simulated environments (Hopper and Overcooked), raising questions about real-world applicability (Section 4.1).", "The comparison with baselines is somewhat limited, particularly in terms of computational efficiency and scalability (Section 4.3)."], "suggestions": ["Include a theoretical analysis of the method's convergence properties to strengthen the technical soundness (Section 3).", "Extend the evaluation to more diverse and real-world environments to demonstrate broader applicability (Section 4).", "Provide a more detailed comparison with state-of-the-art baselines, including computational efficiency metrics (Section 4.3)."], "rating": 7, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}

